      "sampleAudioTranscript": "The talk mainly discusses the relationship between wage growth, consumption, and household debt over the past five years. The main idea is that wage growth has been quite weak, only about 5%, while consumption increased much more, around 15%. First, this gap shows that higher consumption isn’t coming from higher income. Then, I heard about a significant rise in housing debt, nearly 40%, which explains how people are borrowing more to support their spending. An example provided was that most wages go toward paying off this household debt. To sum up, the talk sheds light on how borrowing, rather than wage growth, is driving consumption."
