      "sampleAudioTranscript": "The talk mainly discusses the influence of Morton Prince’s work on dissociation and multiple personalities. First, it explains how Prince popularized the concept of multiple personality disorder through his study of a case called Christine Beauchamp, a pseudonym for Clara Norton Fowler. Then, it highlights how Prince observed that although sharing the same body, her different personalities had unique thoughts, beliefs, and memories. Additionally, he successfully treated her by merging these separate personalities into one. For example, Christine Beauchamp was considered the first documented case of split personality. Overall, the talk showed how Prince’s case shaped our understanding of dissociation and personalities."
