      "sampleAudioTranscript": "The talk mainly discusses a recently found portrait believed to be of Julius Caesar, pulled from the River Rhone. First, the discovery gained a lot of attention, even making it onto National Geographic and French TV. Then, the speaker highlighted that this statue is claimed to be carved during Caesar’s lifetime, which is rare in Roman portrait iconography. Another important point is that its unusual look compared to other Caesar portraits actually supports its authenticity. For example, the underwater archaeologist was convinced it was Caesar right away. Overall, the talk emphasized the portrait’s potential historical significance."
