      "sampleAudioTranscript": "The talk mainly discusses how stunning images of stars, galaxies, and nebulae are created using the Hubble Space Telescope. First, it explains that light from distant objects travels across space and is captured by Hubble’s large mirror. Then, the light is converted into electrical signals by special cameras equipped with CCD chips, similar to digital cameras. Another key point is the challenge of capturing the wide range of brightness in space, which makes it hard to show all details clearly in one image. For example, the Advanced Camera for Surveys uses millions of pixels to handle this. Overall, the talk highlights how complex and fascinating it is to transform light into these amazing cosmic pictures."
