      "transcript": "Yeah, I'd just like to comment on that from the British perspective, because here too, I think a totally irrational new fee policy has just been introduced. \nBefore 2012, UK domestic students paid about £3,000. \nAnd the new Coalition government decided that the way to drive up value for money was to remove direct public subsidy from the universities and instead charge the full fee to the student. That's I think about $4,500 in tuition.\nThey jacked tuition fees up from about 3000 to £9000. \nThat's about $15,000. \nWe're only a few years into this new regime, and already it's becoming apparent that the new regime, despite trebling the burden on students, is going to still cost the taxpayer almost as much as the previous one. \nSo it's it's verifying exactly the sort of notions that you were talking about from the Atlantic and elsewhere that actually are the sort of model that we've been talking about in Switzerland and Sweden and Denmark and to some extent in most of continental Europe, is actually a far better way of generating value for money. \nAnd I think there are lots of other ways of coming to that same conclusion.\n",
