      "shortId": "SST0062",
      "title": "Drop out of school",
      "transcript": "Low achievers, particularly boys, are more likely to drop out of school, with two primary factors contributing to this trend: push factors and pull factors. The pull factor is often economic. In many cases, boys may find it easier to secure jobs that do not require high levels of education, which can create an incentive to leave school early and start working. This is particularly true in certain sectors where manual labor or unskilled work is available and accessible, allowing them to support themselves financially without completing their education. For girls, the situation is different. Due to societal expectations and job market realities, there are fewer employment opportunities available that specifically target girls at a young age. As a result, girls who might consider leaving school early face a more restricted set of options. They are more likely to encounter the push factor, which comes from a lack of opportunities and societal pressures to either remain in school or face the challenges of unemployment or low-paying part-time jobs. Without the same immediate economic incentives to leave school, girls are generally less likely to drop out early compared to boys. This dynamic highlights how gender and economic factors intersect to influence educational outcomes. It also suggests the need for targeted interventions that address both push and pull factors, ensuring that both boys and girls have the support and opportunities to stay in school and complete their education.",
