      "transcript": "A social contract was a popular concept during the 17th and 18th centuries. At that time, it referred to the idea of people stepping out of their natural state. In their natural state, humans are often compared to animals. However, while animals inherently understand the importance of peace for survival, humans are more complex. For humans, whether they choose to act peacefully or fiercely depends on their personal beliefs and decisions. Today, the meaning of the social contract has evolved. It now represents an agreement among people to join together for a common purpose, such as working on a project or building a community. In this modern context, the social contract emphasizes cooperation and mutual trust. People agree not to fight and instead rely on one another to resolve disputes fairly and constructively. This shift in the concept of the social contract reflects how society has changed over time. It highlights the importance of collaboration and trust as essential elements for achieving shared goals and maintaining harmony in a complex world. ",
