      "title": "Women's Leadership",
      "transcript": "Leadership can be understood as a spectrum, encompassing two primary forms: leading from the front and contributing behind the scenes. Historically, men have dominated the more visible, front-facing leadership roles, often viewed as the traditional definition of leadership. Women, on the other hand, have frequently worked behind the scenes, offering essential support, guidance, and decision-making, though often without the recognition they deserved. This dynamic reflected societal expectations and cultural norms of the time. In recent years, however, this paradigm has begun to shift significantly. Women are increasingly stepping into prominent leadership positions, breaking barriers and redefining what it means to lead. From politics and business to science and education, women are not only leading from the front but are also bringing new perspectives, collaboration, and empathy into leadership practices. This evolution underscores the importance of diverse leadership styles, demonstrating that progress is best achieved when all voices are represented. As we embrace this change, it’s clear that the future of leadership will be more inclusive and dynamic, benefiting from the strengths and contributions of everyone.",
