      "shortId": "SST0354",
      "title": "Biological Forgetting ",
      "transcript": "Forgetting is a natural and inevitable part of human life, encompassing experiences, emotions, and thoughts—a process often referred to as biological forgetting. It happens daily, shaping how we interact with the world and how we process new information.Remembering, however, is a much more complex task, and people have long sought ways to overcome the limitations of biological forgetting.Human memory is not a fixed system; it is dynamic and reconstructive, influenced and reshaped by past experiences and perspectives. Throughout history, our ancestors have worked tirelessly to improve memory, from oral storytelling traditions to written records and, more recently, advanced technological tools. These efforts not only preserve knowledge but also help individuals and societies retain a sense of identity, proving that the challenge of memory is as much about innovation as it is about understanding ourselves.",
