      "shortId": "SST0360",
      "title": "SST0360",
      "transcript": "Many scientists, myself included, have long been fascinated by animals emitting light from their bodies, a phenomenon known as bioluminescence. This is usually caused by a chemical reaction, either when under attack or while trying to attract a mate. Through our previous research, we believe that bioluminescence in fish was made by the fish themselves. To produce the light, they needed a specific molecule, luciferin, and an enzyme closely related to it, which forces the molecule to act in this way. We thought that the fish produced these two components internally and independently. However, some of our more recent research has shown that, in fact, one species of fish essentially steals these components from its prey and does not produce either of them itself. Rather than self-production, the fish extracts the necessary ingredients during digestion. the fact that the enzyme and molecule are not destroyed during the digestive process has surprised us the most.  Although this is a breakthrough in biological science, we still have a lot more to discover to fully understand bioluminescence in fish.",
