      "shortId": "SST0364",
      "title": "SST0364",
      "transcript": "A new study on the effects of the rising temperature in the Arctic on plants there shows just how serious the increase is for this particular region. The study shows that the temperature in the Arctic is 0.75 degrees Celsius higher now than it was a decade ago. If you're not sure just how huge an increase that is, let me tell you that the temperature of the Earth has only increased by 0.8 degrees in almost 140 years. The Arctic has been particularly affected by global temperature changes because of its position at the top of the Earth and there have been significant changes to the plants that are found there. The results of the new study show that plants in the Arctic responded positively to the temperature increase by growing taller than they usually would. But the plants' increased height meant they covered less of the ground, so more heat went into the ground, causing it to heat up. The temperature in the region went up even further as a result.",
