      "passage": "One probable motive determining the Viking expansion suggests that the inhabitants had outgrown the agricultural potential of their Scandinavian homeland. For a coastal population with progressive sea-faring expertise, it made sense to expand overseas and birth records for that era reflect an unexpected rise in birth rates. Another explanation is the Vikings’ ability to exploit a moment of weakness in the surrounding territories, capitalising on internal divisions and the lack of organised naval opposition. Viking ships could travel undetected and strike relatively easily given that many settlements were positioned near navigable rivers. The decline in profitability of former trade routes also played a fundamental role. Trade on the Mediterranean Sea had reduced substantially, thus by opening further trade routes, the Vikings profited from international trade by expanding beyond their established borders.",
