      "transcript": "Some of Shakespeare's earlier critics were quite nonplussed by his last plays. Here we have a dramatist who writes a series of history plays reflecting political dilemmas of the day, often highly controversial ones, then goes on to write some of the darkest tragedies in the language. And then, in his last plays, what does he do? He seems to retreat into a kind of second childhood, dreamlike episodic plots, stories of fantastic voyages, magical creatures, miracles. Bored with reality was one critic's conclusion about him. But actually, these plays contain psychological traumas just as deep as anything in the tragedies. Look at the portrait of nurtured grief in Pericles, or that jealousy in the Winter's Tale, which is every bit as destructive as anything in the earlier plays. So there is no way we can say that in these last plays, Shakespeare just turned his back on.",
